WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.480
INTRO:
When we think of the most volatile assets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:08.240
within the financial space, most of us think about&nbsp;
crypto. Despite reaching a market cap of over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:14.880
$1 trillion, Bitcoin still experiences 70 to 80%&nbsp;
crashes on a regular basis. Within the crypto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:20.080
markets, these are actually rather tame crashes&nbsp;
given what happens to certain altcoins like Luna.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.760
Anyway, as you guys probably already know, Bitcoin&nbsp;
is currently experiencing one of these bear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:31.920
markets having sold of 71% since November. The&nbsp;
most ironic part, however, is that Bitcoin has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:35.600
actually held up better than some of the&nbsp;
most reputable and well known businesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:43.120
in the world such as Shopify. Since November,&nbsp;
Shopify is down 82.6%, and let’s not forget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:47.413
Shopify is not some penny stock or options&nbsp;
contract. They were the second biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.413 --> 00:00:52.720
ecommerce player in the world only beaten out by&nbsp;
Amazon. And at the peak, they were $228 billion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.600 --> 00:01:01.200
but now, they’re only worth $38.5 billion. This&nbsp;
means that within 8 months, Shopify has lost $190&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:06.720
billion worth of maket cap. Now of course, you&nbsp;
could argue that the entire market is crashing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:12.080
and that’s true. But, to my knowledge, Shopify&nbsp;
is thus far the worst performing large cap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:16.080
company in the world. So,&nbsp;
what happened to Shopify?

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:21.120
THE DROPSHIPPING BUBBLE:&nbsp;

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:26.080
One of the driving forces of Shopify over the&nbsp;
past few years has been the dropshipping bubble.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:30.400
I mean, how many times have you heard&nbsp;
about teens making 6 if not 7 figures on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:35.280
Shopify every year. And, let’s not forget about&nbsp;
all those dropshipping and Amazon FBA gurus on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:40.480
YouTube. Now, I’m not trying to say that all of&nbsp;
these guys are scams or that dropshipping doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:45.280
work. Dropshipping is absolutely a viable business&nbsp;
model and people who really know the business will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:50.640
do well regardless of how competitive it gets.&nbsp;
With that being said though, the vast majority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.840
of people who are getting into dropshipping&nbsp;
aren’t looking to start a series business.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:59.200
A lot of them are trying to make some quick&nbsp;
money on the side, and this was especially true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:03.520
at the beginning of the pandemic. In&nbsp;
April and May of 2020, the interest in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:09.200
shopify literally doubled. And it wasn’t just&nbsp;
entrepreneurs that were interested in Shopify.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:14.240
Investors were also quite intrigued by the idea.&nbsp;
Most investors didn’t want to start their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:18.160
stores or anything, but they were quick to&nbsp;
identify all of the extra interest heading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:23.440
towards Shopify. And as hopeful entrepreneurs&nbsp;
piled on on the customer side, investors piled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:28.240
on on the investor side, and it wasn’t long&nbsp;
till Shopify stock was sent to the moon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:33.600
From the pandemic bottom, Shopify stock would&nbsp;
grow nearly 500% within a matter of months.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:39.440
While this likely felt super rewarding to Shopify&nbsp;
investors, this was also completely unsustainable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:45.680
Shopify’s PE ratio or price to earnings ratio&nbsp;
went to extreme levels of over 600. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:50.720
it should be noted that Shopify has historically&nbsp;
reinvested the vast majority of their profits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:56.560
so their net income is abnormally low which&nbsp;
skews their PE ratio, but this does not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:03.440
apply to their PS ratio. Shopify’s PS ratio or&nbsp;
Price to Sales ratio also skyrocketed to 53.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:09.600
To put that in perspective, even Tesla only had&nbsp;
a peak PS ratio of 24 meaning that according to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:15.680
this metric, Shopify was valued twice as richly&nbsp;
as Tesla. Clearly, this was a hyper valuation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:21.120
that was just waiting to crash, and that’s exactly&nbsp;
what would happen as the new interest in Shopify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:26.480
fell off a cliff. Here’s the thing, most people&nbsp;
who try dropshipping don’t actually make it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:31.440
In fact, nearly 90% of dropshippers quit within&nbsp;
the first month after not making as much money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:36.480
as they originally envisioned. This number&nbsp;
rises to 96% if we zoom out to the first 6&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:41.040
months of business. Out of the remaining 4%,&nbsp;
it’s likely that a good portion just continue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:46.080
to treat dropshipping as a side business. They&nbsp;
might make 2 or 3 thousand dollars every month&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.720
which is great supplemental income, but not quite&nbsp;
enough to quit your job over especially given how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:56.000
volatile dropshipping income can be. Considering&nbsp;
this, only about 1 to 2% of dropshippers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:59.280
actually end up becoming long term customers&nbsp;
of Shopify who make them a lot of money. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:03.280
this becomes abundantly clear when we take&nbsp;
a look back at the google trends graph.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:09.120
The new interest in Shopify died off as quickly as&nbsp;
it went up. And as the hype amongst entrepreneurs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:13.840
died off, it was just a matter of time until&nbsp;
the hype amongst investors also died off.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:17.680
AWFUL QUARTERS:&nbsp;

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:22.480
As new entreprenuer interest died off, it was only&nbsp;
natural that Shopify’s financials reflected that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:27.840
sooner or later, and that’s exactly what’s been&nbsp;
happening over the past few quarters. Revenue and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:32.320
profit growth has slowed down, and Shopify expects&nbsp;
these metrics to slow down even more in the coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:38.000
quarters. Hearing that, you’d probably think that&nbsp;
Shopify’s revenue was down 50% or something, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:43.520
that’s not actually the case. In fact, Shopify’s&nbsp;
12 month revenue and gross profit are still higher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:49.760
than ever before. Not to mention, neither of these&nbsp;
metrics have ever gone down on a 12 month basis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:54.800
But, investors are fearful that Shopify is close&nbsp;
to posting their first year over year contraction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:58.960
If we take a closer look at Shopify’s year over&nbsp;
year growth in gross profit, you’ll see that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:05.120
growth rate is lower than we’ve ever seen. Shopify&nbsp;
has historically posted 80 to 90% growth quarter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:11.520
after quarter, but today, this growth has dwindled&nbsp;
down to just 14%. Now, I know this makes a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:15.440
of people in the comments quite frustrated&nbsp;
because it’s completely unrealistic to expect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:20.480
that a company grows forever. 14% year over year&nbsp;
growth is still a great number from a fundamental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:25.120
perspective. But the thing to keep in mind is&nbsp;
that the stock price had priced in over 50% annual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:31.040
growth for years to come. So, a mere 14% was&nbsp;
simply not enough to appease investors. Something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:35.840
else to note is that Shopify’s financials aren’t&nbsp;
as peachy when we take a look at their net income.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:41.360
Shopify’s net income has never been particularly&nbsp;
high given that they just reinvest everything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:46.400
but since the end of 2020, Shopify’s net income&nbsp;
was finally starting to reach respectable levels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:53.840
until the last 2 quarters. During these quarters,&nbsp;
Shopify lost $371 million and then $1.47 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:57.760
These losses can be traced back to&nbsp;
a couple of factors starting with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:03.040
increasing operational expenses. Shopify is&nbsp;
spending significantly more across the board.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:08.240
Sales and marketing has increased from&nbsp;
$186 million to $303 million. Research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:14.320
and Development has increased from $175 million&nbsp;
to also $303 million. General and administrative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:20.080
have increased from $67 million to $108 million.&nbsp;
And finally, transaction and loan losses have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:25.280
increased from $10 million to $20 million. As you&nbsp;
can see, all of these operational categories have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:32.160
increased by atleast 60%. Meanwhile, revenue has&nbsp;
only increased from $988 million to $1.203 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:37.440
or roughly 21%. It seems that Shopify has yet to&nbsp;
account for diminishing growth, and that they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:42.080
spending as if they were still growing at 70 to&nbsp;
80% per year. Aside from increasing operational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:46.400
expenses, Shopify has also gotten destroyed&nbsp;
with their investments given that they’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:50.960
mostly invested in hyper growth companies like&nbsp;
Affirm, Global-e, and Silvergate Capital which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:56.320
are all down 70 to 90%. Such investments were the&nbsp;
driving factor behind Shopify’s external loses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:01.840
of $1.55 billion last quarter which left&nbsp;
them with a horrendous quarterly report.

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:05.600
FUNDAMENTAL ISSUES:&nbsp;

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:09.920
Aside from these superficial factors that are&nbsp;
affecting Shopify’s stock price and bottom line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:16.000
Shopify has a few fundamental issues as well&nbsp;
starting with dropshipping. Today, Shopify has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:20.480
basically become synonymous with dropshipping,&nbsp;
and honestly, it’s probably where a significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:25.680
portion of their revenue comes from. But, Shopify&nbsp;
was never created to support dropshipping.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:30.400
The purpose of Shopify was to make it easier for&nbsp;
small merchants to set up an online store. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:35.280
dropshippers aren’t merchants at all. They’re&nbsp;
actually just marketers who route orders to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:40.480
Ali express. This might not sound like a big&nbsp;
deal at first glance, but it is a big deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:45.840
for two reasons. First of all, people associate&nbsp;
Shopify with fake gurus and get rich schemes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:50.320
And secondly, the products that are sold on&nbsp;
Shopify are garbage quality because all the money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:55.360
is being spent on marketing. Now, I’m not&nbsp;
against marketing by any means. Spending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.360 --> 00:08:00.240
20% of your revenue on getting new sales makes&nbsp;
perfect sense as a business. But, dropshippers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:05.840
generally spend over 50% of the revenue on&nbsp;
marketing. If we have a $50 product for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:10.720
this is usually how the breakdown goes. The&nbsp;
dropshipper spends $10 on the product itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:16.160
they spend $30 on marketing, and then they&nbsp;
keep $10 for themselves. Because of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:20.560
a $50 product from Shopify is usually not even&nbsp;
comparable to a $50 product from a physical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:25.680
retailer in terms of quality and longevity,&nbsp;
and consumers are starting to notice. Recently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:29.920
I posted a video about how physical retailers&nbsp;
are making a comeback against online retailers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:33.440
and one of the top complaints that I saw in&nbsp;
the comment section was that goods from online&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:38.480
retailers are simply trash quality. This user&nbsp;
commented “I’d like to add that as of today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:43.600
on Amazon, most categories are flooded with the&nbsp;
same chinese low quality products but rebranded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:48.640
so for a lot of items it’s just quicker to find&nbsp;
them in a store”. And this guy was just talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:54.320
about Amazon. The quality problem is exponentially&nbsp;
worse on Shopify, and this is no doubt ruining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.320 --> 00:08:59.200
their brand image. One way that Shopify is trying&nbsp;
to move away from dropshipping is by growing their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:03.840
own logistical services. They’re hoping that by&nbsp;
offering small businesses logistical capabilities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:08.240
they can attract higher quality clients. With&nbsp;
the help of Shopify, these small businesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:11.600
would finally have the opportunity to offer&nbsp;
shipping times and prices that are competitive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:17.520
with Amazon and Walmart. The only problem with&nbsp;
this is that its basically just Amazon FBA.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:23.200
The idea itself isn’t bad per se, and I’m not&nbsp;
really worried about Shopify being able to attract&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:27.840
enough customers. The real problem, however,&nbsp;
is that developing something like Amazon FBA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:33.200
is extraordinarily expensive. I mean,&nbsp;
Amazon launched the FBA program in 2006,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:38.480
and they’ve spent the past 16 years refining&nbsp;
every portion of the business. But despite this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:43.200
Amazon still posted an operational loss for&nbsp;
their retail business last quarter. So, clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:48.400
if Shopify doubles down on logistics, they’re not&nbsp;
going to be posting sizable profits anytime soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:51.280
meaning that the stock may still&nbsp;
have a lot of room to the downside.

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:55.440
THE FUTURE OF SHOPIFY:&nbsp;

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:59.840
I know this video has been extremely negative&nbsp;
towards Shopify, but I don’t want to give off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:05.280
the impression that Shopify is struggling per se.&nbsp;
Yes, their stock price has gotten obliterated and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:08.987
they’re experiencing some painful quarters due to&nbsp;
rising operational expenses and some large write&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.987 --> 00:10:13.920
offs . But, in the grand scheme of things, Shopify&nbsp;
is still an ecommerce giant and they are the go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:18.400
to service for creating online stores. With that&nbsp;
being said though, a lot of their growth in recent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:23.360
years has come from dropshipping which is not&nbsp;
the best for their long term business. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:27.360
I don’t think Shopify wants to be&nbsp;
an American gateway to Ali express.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:31.760
Their real goal is to become small merchants’ best&nbsp;
friend, and that’s exactly what they’re trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:37.200
do with their logistical services. But this is&nbsp;
by no means a small undertaking, and it’ll take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:42.000
several years for such a plan to produce returns.&nbsp;
In the meantime, Shopify will likely post little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:48.080
to no profits. And in our current risk off market,&nbsp;
that’s basically a death sentence and I wouldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:53.440
be surprised if Shopify ends up selling off 90-95%&nbsp;
before finally bottoming. If you’re willing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:57.440
hold through this potential short term volatility&nbsp;
however, Shopify will likely be a phenomenal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.440 --> 00:11:03.440
investment 10 years down the line, but that’s just&nbsp;
what I think and it’s not financial advice. Are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:08.400
you guys a fan of dropshipping? Comment that down&nbsp;
below. Also, drop a like if you wish that online&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:12.880
products weren’t garbage quality. And of course&nbsp;
consider joining our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:16.320
future video ideas and consider subscribing&nbsp;
to see more questions logically answered.

